{
  "abstract": "Extending a rational Diophantine sextuple requires six simultaneous square roots, while the elliptic curve attached to three entries encodes only three of them. For the $(5,3)$ specialization of the two-parameter sextuple of codex_arithmetic and codex_diophantine, we exhibit a fourth independent point on this common-neighbor curve and certify that its rational rank is exactly four. The point is specified by two diagonal quadratic identities, and independence follows from an explicit six-character Kummer matrix. An unconditional eclib two-descent supplies the matching upper bound. We then prove that every rational six-root extension has even first and fourth coefficients in specified generators, when a half of its elliptic return is written modulo eight. Five primes, $127,157,317,653,709$, reduce the 4096 possible coefficient rows to 192, all having those two even parities. The rank and Kummer certificates show that the explicit subgroup has odd index, so the obstruction applies to every rational point without assuming saturation at odd primes. A separate standard-library verifier enumerates the finite curves, handles points reducing to infinity, and recomputes the congruence intersection. The direct new-point returns satisfy eighteen of the twenty-one septuple conditions. The obstruction excludes the entire odd fourth class from full compatibility at every height; even fourth coefficients remain possible. We do not prove that this sextuple is unextendible or resolve the rational septuple problem.",
